1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,791
Your phone buzzes. It's a reply to the long

2
00:00:03,831 --> 00:00:06,760
careful message you sent an hour ago.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,088
And it says… okay. Just okay.

4
00:00:09,128 --> 00:00:10,601
With a period.

5
00:00:11,651 --> 00:00:13,093
So you read it again.

6
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:15,070
And again. Are they annoyed?

7
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:16,905
Did I say something wrong?

8
00:00:16,945 --> 00:00:18,246
And why the period?

9
00:00:18,286 --> 00:00:21,422
Within a minute, two letters have turned into

10
00:00:21,462 --> 00:00:24,528
a whole story… and the story is never a good

11
00:00:24,568 --> 00:00:25,200
one.

12
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:27,561
Here's the strange part.

13
00:00:27,601 --> 00:00:30,151
Psychologists have tested how well people read

14
00:00:30,191 --> 00:00:31,502
tone in a typed message.

15
00:00:31,542 --> 00:00:33,754
And the answer says a lot less about the

16
00:00:33,794 --> 00:00:36,344
message… and a lot more about the brain that's

17
00:00:36,384 --> 00:00:37,353
reading it.

18
00:00:38,403 --> 00:00:41,002
So why does your brain turn a two letter text

19
00:00:41,042 --> 00:00:43,407
into a verdict? How often is that verdict

20
00:00:43,447 --> 00:00:45,929
right? And what should you do the next time

21
00:00:45,969 --> 00:00:48,783
okay, with a period, lands on your screen?

22
00:00:54,633 --> 00:00:57,207
In two thousand five, a team led by the

23
00:00:57,247 --> 00:01:00,290
psychologists Justin Kruger and Nicholas Epley

24
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:03,038
ran a set of experiments on exactly this.

25
00:01:03,078 --> 00:01:06,122
In one of them, sixty students at Cornell came

26
00:01:06,162 --> 00:01:07,383
in, in pairs.

27
00:01:08,433 --> 00:01:10,757
Each person got a list of short sentences

28
00:01:10,797 --> 00:01:12,718
about campus life. Some were meant

29
00:01:12,758 --> 00:01:15,082
sarcastically. Some were meant seriously.

30
00:01:15,122 --> 00:01:17,619
Their job was to get the real meaning across

31
00:01:17,659 --> 00:01:18,989
to their partner.

32
00:01:20,039 --> 00:01:22,422
Here's the twist. Half of them typed their

33
00:01:22,462 --> 00:01:23,806
sentences into an email.

34
00:01:23,846 --> 00:01:26,402
The other half read them out loud into a tape

35
00:01:26,442 --> 00:01:27,844
recorder. Same sentences.

36
00:01:27,884 --> 00:01:29,272
Different channel.

37
00:01:30,322 --> 00:01:32,717
Before the partners saw anything, the senders

38
00:01:32,757 --> 00:01:34,990
predicted how many their partner would get

39
00:01:35,030 --> 00:01:37,209
right. And the email senders were just as

40
00:01:37,249 --> 00:01:39,872
confident as the people using their voice.

41
00:01:40,922 --> 00:01:43,447
The listeners got close to three out of four

42
00:01:43,487 --> 00:01:44,554
right. The readers?

43
00:01:44,594 --> 00:01:47,626
Their accuracy was no better than a coin flip.

44
00:01:48,676 --> 00:01:50,363
And the readers had no idea.

45
00:01:50,403 --> 00:01:53,014
They felt about as sure of their answers as

46
00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:55,605
the listeners did. Nearly nine out of ten

47
00:01:55,645 --> 00:01:58,195
they believed… with the accuracy of a coin

48
00:01:58,235 --> 00:01:58,893
flip.

49
00:01:59,943 --> 00:02:02,381
So the problem isn't that text is hard to

50
00:02:02,421 --> 00:02:04,860
read. The problem is that it doesn't feel

51
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:07,580
hard. Your brain hands you an interpretation...

52
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:09,360
and stamps it, certain.

53
00:02:10,410 --> 00:02:13,468
Even in their smallest study, where most

54
00:02:13,508 --> 00:02:16,721
messages did get through, senders expected

55
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:19,587
ninety seven percent of them to land.

56
00:02:19,627 --> 00:02:21,445
Eighty four percent did.

57
00:02:21,485 --> 00:02:23,307
Confident… and off.

58
00:02:24,357 --> 00:02:26,728
But surely this is about strangers, right?

59
00:02:26,768 --> 00:02:29,310
Your best friend's texts, you can read those.

60
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:31,996
The researchers wondered the same thing.

61
00:02:33,046 --> 00:02:36,566
So they ran a bigger version at the University

62
00:02:36,606 --> 00:02:40,125
of Illinois. One hundred and fifty four pairs.

63
00:02:40,165 --> 00:02:41,673
Some were strangers.

64
00:02:41,713 --> 00:02:45,232
Some were friends. And this time, the messages

65
00:02:45,272 --> 00:02:48,250
carried sarcasm, seriousness, anger, or

66
00:02:48,290 --> 00:02:49,259
sadness.

67
00:02:50,309 --> 00:02:52,750
Over email, the readers believed they had

68
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:55,110
understood almost nine in ten messages.

69
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:57,470
They had actually got about six in ten.

70
00:02:57,510 --> 00:02:59,406
And here's the part that stings...

71
00:02:59,446 --> 00:03:02,519
friends were no more accurate than strangers.

72
00:03:03,569 --> 00:03:06,207
Knowing someone for years didn't fix it.

73
00:03:06,247 --> 00:03:07,814
Which raises a question.

74
00:03:07,854 --> 00:03:09,822
If the tone isn't in the text...

75
00:03:09,862 --> 00:03:12,622
where is your brain getting it from?

76
00:03:16,272 --> 00:03:18,916
Think about what a voice carries.

77
00:03:18,956 --> 00:03:22,250
Pitch. Pauses. A little laugh at the end.

78
00:03:22,290 --> 00:03:25,341
A face adds a raised eyebrow, a smile.

79
00:03:25,381 --> 00:03:28,659
Text strips almost all of that away.

80
00:03:29,709 --> 00:03:31,832
But your brain doesn't like a gap.

81
00:03:31,872 --> 00:03:34,694
So it fills it. And the voice it uses to fill

82
00:03:34,734 --> 00:03:35,911
it… is yours.

83
00:03:36,961 --> 00:03:39,312
Kruger and Epley saw this from the sender's

84
00:03:39,352 --> 00:03:41,869
side. When you type a sarcastic line, you hear

85
00:03:41,909 --> 00:03:44,149
the sarcasm in your head as you write it.

86
00:03:44,189 --> 00:03:45,261
So it seems obvious.

87
00:03:45,301 --> 00:03:46,040
To you.

88
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:50,329
In one study, they asked senders to read their

89
00:03:50,369 --> 00:03:53,322
own messages out loud in the opposite tone

90
00:03:53,362 --> 00:03:56,031
first. Sarcastic lines read seriously.

91
00:03:56,071 --> 00:03:58,773
Serious lines read sarcastically.

92
00:03:59,823 --> 00:04:01,771
And their overconfidence disappeared.

93
00:04:01,811 --> 00:04:04,242
Once they heard the message the way a stranger

94
00:04:04,282 --> 00:04:06,390
might hear it, their predictions finally

95
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:07,640
matched reality.

96
00:04:08,690 --> 00:04:09,824
Now flip that around.

97
00:04:09,864 --> 00:04:12,227
When a text lands on your phone, you're the

98
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:13,569
one supplying the voice.

99
00:04:13,609 --> 00:04:16,140
And the voice you supply depends on your mood

100
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:17,817
your history with that person...

101
00:04:17,857 --> 00:04:20,107
and what you're already afraid of.

102
00:04:21,157 --> 00:04:23,937
If you've been worrying that a friend is upset

103
00:04:23,977 --> 00:04:26,635
with you, the voice in your head reads okay...

104
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:27,515
as cold.

105
00:04:28,565 --> 00:04:31,021
There's a reason that voice so often sounds

106
00:04:31,061 --> 00:04:33,518
negative. In a two thousand eight review of

107
00:04:33,558 --> 00:04:36,072
how emotion travels by email, the management

108
00:04:36,112 --> 00:04:38,627
researcher Kristin Byron argued that readers

109
00:04:38,667 --> 00:04:41,297
tend to see messages as more negative, or more

110
00:04:41,337 --> 00:04:43,487
neutral, than the sender meant.

111
00:04:44,537 --> 00:04:47,459
That was a theory paper, built on earlier

112
00:04:47,499 --> 00:04:49,771
studies, not one big experiment.

113
00:04:49,811 --> 00:04:52,878
But small pieces of evidence point the same

114
00:04:52,918 --> 00:04:54,857
way. Take that period.

115
00:04:55,907 --> 00:04:59,039
In a study at Binghamton University, a hundred

116
00:04:59,079 --> 00:05:02,072
and twenty six students read short exchanges

117
00:05:02,112 --> 00:05:05,037
where the reply was one word, like okay, or

118
00:05:05,077 --> 00:05:08,002
yup. When the reply was a text message, the

119
00:05:08,042 --> 00:05:11,035
version ending in a period was rated as less

120
00:05:11,075 --> 00:05:11,977
sincere.

121
00:05:13,027 --> 00:05:15,685
On a handwritten note, the period made no

122
00:05:15,725 --> 00:05:18,448
difference. The authors called it a modest

123
00:05:18,488 --> 00:05:20,159
study, with one age group.

124
00:05:20,199 --> 00:05:23,054
But it shows how much meaning we pour into a

125
00:05:23,094 --> 00:05:24,168
single dot.

126
00:05:25,218 --> 00:05:26,879
So your brain fills the gap.

127
00:05:26,919 --> 00:05:29,552
And it tends to fill it with something worse

128
00:05:29,592 --> 00:05:30,707
than what was sent.

129
00:05:30,747 --> 00:05:33,258
Which leads to the most useful question in

130
00:05:33,298 --> 00:05:35,749
this whole video. When your brain guesses

131
00:05:35,789 --> 00:05:38,448
wrong… which way does it usually lean?

132
00:05:42,098 --> 00:05:43,454
Here's where it turns.

133
00:05:43,494 --> 00:05:46,374
If our errors were random, we'd guess too warm

134
00:05:46,414 --> 00:05:48,088
about as often as too cold.

135
00:05:48,128 --> 00:05:51,334
But in one area, the errors have a direction.

136
00:05:52,384 --> 00:05:54,352
In a two thousand eighteen study, the

137
00:05:54,392 --> 00:05:56,522
psychologists Erica Boothby, Gus Cooney

138
00:05:56,562 --> 00:05:58,856
Gillian Sandstrom and Margaret Clark paired

139
00:05:58,896 --> 00:06:00,429
strangers for a conversation.

140
00:06:00,469 --> 00:06:02,763
Afterwards, each person rated how much they

141
00:06:02,803 --> 00:06:03,848
liked their partner...

142
00:06:03,888 --> 00:06:06,734
and guessed how much their partner liked them.

143
00:06:07,784 --> 00:06:10,160
People consistently underestimated it.

144
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:12,893
Their partners liked them more, and enjoyed

145
00:06:12,933 --> 00:06:15,309
their company more, than they thought.

146
00:06:15,349 --> 00:06:18,305
The researchers called it the liking gap.

147
00:06:19,355 --> 00:06:21,639
And it wasn't just one awkward lab chat.

148
00:06:21,679 --> 00:06:24,194
They found it in first year students getting

149
00:06:24,234 --> 00:06:26,750
to know their dorm mates, and the gap lasted

150
00:06:26,790 --> 00:06:29,074
for months. They found it in adults at a

151
00:06:29,114 --> 00:06:31,439
personal development workshop too.

152
00:06:32,489 --> 00:06:34,705
Why? The researchers' best guess is that

153
00:06:34,745 --> 00:06:37,186
during a conversation we're busy judging our

154
00:06:37,226 --> 00:06:39,780
own performance, so we miss the signs that the

155
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:42,256
other person is actually enjoying it.

156
00:06:43,306 --> 00:06:45,563
Now think about what that means for the okay

157
00:06:45,603 --> 00:06:47,912
on your screen. You're reading a message from

158
00:06:47,952 --> 00:06:50,001
someone who, on average, probably thinks

159
00:06:50,041 --> 00:06:51,957
better of you than you assume.

160
00:06:53,007 --> 00:06:55,418
And there's a second gap, about reaching out.

161
00:06:55,458 --> 00:06:57,759
In a series of experiments published in two

162
00:06:57,799 --> 00:06:59,828
thousand twenty two, Peggy Liu and her

163
00:06:59,868 --> 00:07:02,224
colleagues had people send a short note or a

164
00:07:02,264 --> 00:07:05,065
small gift to someone in their social circle.

165
00:07:06,115 --> 00:07:08,537
The senders guessed how much it would be

166
00:07:08,577 --> 00:07:11,245
appreciated. Then the people who got it said

167
00:07:11,285 --> 00:07:12,660
how they actually felt.

168
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:15,820
Over and over, the senders underestimated it.

169
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:19,500
And the gap was biggest when the message was a

170
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:21,823
surprise… like a text out of the blue to

171
00:07:21,863 --> 00:07:24,203
someone you haven't talked to in a while.

172
00:07:24,243 --> 00:07:26,857
The exact text people keep not sending.

173
00:07:27,907 --> 00:07:29,941
Put it together. Text hides tone.

174
00:07:29,981 --> 00:07:32,580
Your brain fills in the missing voice with

175
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:35,471
your fears. And on the question of what people

176
00:07:35,511 --> 00:07:38,236
think of you, those fears tend to run darker

177
00:07:38,276 --> 00:07:39,569
than the truth.

178
00:07:40,619 --> 00:07:42,859
One honest caveat. These are averages.

179
00:07:42,899 --> 00:07:45,560
Sometimes okay with a period really does mean

180
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:48,080
someone's upset. The point isn't that your

181
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:49,401
worry is always wrong.

182
00:07:49,441 --> 00:07:51,621
It's that your confidence in it isn't

183
00:07:51,661 --> 00:07:52,551
evidence.

184
00:07:56,201 --> 00:07:58,619
So what do you do when a short text sends your

185
00:07:58,659 --> 00:08:00,916
brain spinning? Here are three things, each

186
00:08:00,956 --> 00:08:02,642
one tied to the research.

187
00:08:03,692 --> 00:08:06,035
One. Read it again, in a different voice.

188
00:08:06,075 --> 00:08:08,592
Remember the senders who read their messages

189
00:08:08,632 --> 00:08:11,149
in the opposite tone, and suddenly saw how a

190
00:08:11,189 --> 00:08:12,992
stranger might hear them?

191
00:08:14,042 --> 00:08:15,491
Do the reader's version.

192
00:08:15,531 --> 00:08:17,849
Read okay in the warmest voice you can

193
00:08:17,889 --> 00:08:20,331
imagine. Then in a rushed voice, someone

194
00:08:20,371 --> 00:08:21,696
typing at a red light.

195
00:08:21,736 --> 00:08:24,488
If all of them fit the words, the words alone

196
00:08:24,528 --> 00:08:26,925
don't tell you which one is true.

197
00:08:27,975 --> 00:08:29,977
That's not pretending everything is fine.

198
00:08:30,017 --> 00:08:32,119
It's noticing that the cold version was one

199
00:08:32,159 --> 00:08:33,264
reading out of several...

200
00:08:33,304 --> 00:08:34,800
and that you picked it.

201
00:08:35,850 --> 00:08:37,685
Two. If it matters, ask.

202
00:08:37,725 --> 00:08:40,810
A quick, light question, like, all good?

203
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:44,403
clears up in ten seconds what your brain could

204
00:08:44,443 --> 00:08:47,996
spin on for hours. In the Illinois study, even

205
00:08:48,036 --> 00:08:50,106
friends misread each other.

206
00:08:50,146 --> 00:08:51,590
Asking isn't needy.

207
00:08:51,630 --> 00:08:53,464
It's just accurate.

208
00:08:54,514 --> 00:08:57,687
Three. When it really matters, use your voice.

209
00:08:57,727 --> 00:09:00,620
In two thousand twenty one, Amit Kumar and

210
00:09:00,660 --> 00:09:03,553
Nicholas Epley asked two hundred people to

211
00:09:03,593 --> 00:09:06,626
reconnect with an old friend, by email or by

212
00:09:06,666 --> 00:09:07,435
phone.

213
00:09:08,485 --> 00:09:10,933
People expected a phone call to feel more

214
00:09:10,973 --> 00:09:13,177
awkward, so they leaned toward email.

215
00:09:13,217 --> 00:09:15,968
But the people who were assigned to phone felt

216
00:09:16,008 --> 00:09:18,577
a stronger bond. And the phone calls didn't

217
00:09:18,617 --> 00:09:20,302
feel any more awkward.

218
00:09:21,352 --> 00:09:24,265
In another study, strangers chatted by text

219
00:09:24,305 --> 00:09:25,742
by audio, or by video.

220
00:09:25,782 --> 00:09:28,830
Hearing each other's voice made them feel more

221
00:09:28,870 --> 00:09:32,240
connected. Adding video didn't add much more.

222
00:09:33,290 --> 00:09:35,549
So the voice is the missing piece.

223
00:09:35,589 --> 00:09:38,388
Put it back, and most of the guessing goes

224
00:09:38,428 --> 00:09:39,116
away.

225
00:09:40,166 --> 00:09:42,887
And a bonus for when you're the sender.

226
00:09:42,927 --> 00:09:46,003
If a message could be read two ways, add the

227
00:09:46,043 --> 00:09:49,118
tone yourself. A few warm words, a ha, or an

228
00:09:49,158 --> 00:09:52,765
emoji does the job your voice would have done.

229
00:09:53,815 --> 00:09:55,255
None of this is therapy.

230
00:09:55,295 --> 00:09:57,907
If messages regularly leave you anxious for

231
00:09:57,947 --> 00:10:00,313
days, that's worth talking through with

232
00:10:00,353 --> 00:10:02,842
someone. But for the everyday okay, these

233
00:10:02,882 --> 00:10:04,897
three habits go a long way.

234
00:10:05,947 --> 00:10:08,186
So, back to that okay, with a period.

235
00:10:08,226 --> 00:10:10,404
Your brain heard it in a cold voice...

236
00:10:10,444 --> 00:10:13,177
because it had to pick a voice, and it picked

237
00:10:13,217 --> 00:10:15,230
the one you were afraid of.

238
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:18,853
The research says that voice is often wrong

239
00:10:18,893 --> 00:10:21,050
and wrong in a predictable direction.

240
00:10:21,090 --> 00:10:23,782
The person who sent it probably likes you more

241
00:10:23,822 --> 00:10:26,725
than you think… and was probably just busy.

242
00:10:27,775 --> 00:10:29,233
So tell me in the comments.

243
00:10:29,273 --> 00:10:31,675
What's the shortest text that ever sent your

244
00:10:31,715 --> 00:10:32,785
brain into a spiral?

245
00:10:32,825 --> 00:10:35,004
And if you like your psychology with the

246
00:10:35,044 --> 00:10:36,114
receipts, subscribe.

247
00:10:36,154 --> 00:10:38,169
There's a new page every week.
